## 3. 14-Day Study & Build Plan (1 Hour Per Day)
Split each daily 60-minute session into **20 minutes of reading concepts** and **40 minutes of hands-on coding**.

| Day | Focus Topic | 1-Hour Actionable Goal |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Day 1** | **FastAPI Setup** | Install `fastapi` and `uvicorn`. Write a 10-line Hello World API and test it via `/docs`. |
| **Day 2** | **Pydantic Basics** | Create a `BaseModel` class with type hints and build one `POST` route validating JSON input. |
| **Day 3** | **SQLite + SQLAlchemy** | Connect Python to a local `test.db` file and define one SQLAlchemy table class. |
| **Day 4** | **FastAPI + SQLite CRUD** | Connect `POST` and `GET` endpoints to SQLite to save and retrieve live records. |
| **Day 5** | **MySQL Setup** | Install MySQL Server/Workbench locally and create an empty database named `project_db`. |
| **Day 6** | **FastAPI + MySQL** | Install `pymysql`, configure a second SQLAlchemy engine for MySQL, and insert a test row. |
| **Day 7** | **Streamlit UI Basics** | Install `streamlit` and build a standalone page using `st.title`, `st.text_input`, and `st.button`. |
| **Day 8** | **Forms & Dataframes** | Practice grouping inputs inside `st.form` and rendering records using `st.dataframe`. |
| **Day 9** | **Frontend-Backend Link** | Use Python's `requests` library inside Streamlit to send form data to your running FastAPI server. |
| **Day 10** | **Project Backend Build** | Assemble the complete `backend.py` for your chosen project with MySQL and SQLite tables. |
| **Day 11** | **Project Frontend Build** | Write `frontend.py` in Streamlit and verify creating, reading, and updating/deleting records. |
| **Day 12** | **Testing & Edge Cases** | Test invalid inputs (negative numbers, empty fields) to verify Pydantic error responses. |
| **Day 13** | **GitHub Repository** | Add `requirements.txt`, `.env`, and a clear `README.md`, then push your project to GitHub. |
| **Day 14** | **Interview Rehearsal** | Practice a 2-minute walkthrough of your project architecture and "Why" design decisions out loud. |
